<p>Part IV runs from printed page 31 through page 41: four titled stories threaded through with six of the book's untitled "Third Stool from the Rear" barroom vignettes. This is the densest Part for hard content: a bar brawl over a stolen watch, a severed finger, an explicit account of one man repeatedly striking his wife before she fires a pistol and wounds a bystander, two boys who kill marmots with dynamite as a game, a rancher's mercy killing of his own dog after a tractor accident, and a poached bighorn ram. This site's own part division and heading numbers are noted in the book's introduction on the main page for "Third Stool from the Rear."</p>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="third-stool-p31">Third Stool from the Rear</h2>

  <p>"You know that little guy they called Hap who was always whistling along with Peggy when she played the piano in the Rainbow? Sure you do, he trammed for Howard on 7 level for a month or two then worked with Ball in 57 Raise. Well, he won't be whistling through his teeth for a long time.</p>

  <p>"George and Big Jack were arguing over the bar about something and Jack let him have it then come around before he could get to his feet and flopped on him...Jack's too fat to fight but they wrestled from the bar to the can and George never did get to his feet...they called it a draw after awhile and Jack popped for the house. Hap and his wife were in a booth with another couple...you ever see his wife?...she looks 40 years older than him...that gal's really been through the mill...anyway, while George and Jack were wrestling George's wrist watch flew off and landed near one of the booths...Hap scooped it up, quick like, and stuck it in his pocket but Dolly was watching him...next day George come in looking for his watch and Dolly told him where he could find it...he had Zero drive him down to where Hap was living...right across from Snuffy's place and nearly knocked the door down...told the old gal to send Hap out with his watch...soon as he got out he told George he was going to get it back to him...George says 'like hell you were' and fed him a knuckle sandwich...only hit him once but he knocked him ass over teacup...Zero said he spit out two teeth when he was on his hands and knees and a couple more were so loose his wife ended up pulling them. Sort of felt sorry for him...never heard anyone that could whistle like him...he'll think twice before he pockets anything again."</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 31.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="better-knock-the-next-time-you-drop-in">"Better Knock the Next Time You Drop In"</h2>

  <p>Vernon "Whitey" Miller and I were driving parallel drifts on the Robinson claim, 500 level. We worked the same shift and blasted at the same time so there was never any real danger of rattling rocks on one another but it appeared that my vein was making a gentle turn toward Whitey's drift.</p>

  <p>One day, as I was waiting for empty cars to finish mucking out, I thought I could hear cars running on rail through a fracture between our working places. This worried me. Sound travels a long way in hard rock and the sound of a drill bit chattering in siliceous rhyolite will travel well over a hundred feet if a person listens carefully. The sounds I heard from the fracture were much closer.</p>

  <p>In total darkness a person can orient himself in a mine by sounds alone. In 1948 I was working by myself on 7 level of the Amethyst and lost my carbide lamp when it slipped off my hardboil hat as I was dumping a load of ore in a hoist pocket. I went on hands and knees to my jacket where I had a few matches and tried to find a candle, kept for such emergencies, on a makeshift shelf.</p>

  <p>I used up the matches trying to find the candle and had no choice but to try to find my way out. Our minds memorize a lot we are unaware of. I was able to walk upright for much of the way by the feel of the ties before dripping water alerted me to the nearness of 3 shaft where I found the ladder and climbed to 5 level. Once on 5 level and past the pillar supporting 3 shaft I could see light at the end of the tunnel and knew John Nelson would have a spare lamp for me.</p>

  <p>When I was certain I could hear sounds from Whitey's drift through the fracture I told the foreman we were getting too close for comfort. He didn't believe me but consented to come in with a Brunton compass and see if the vein I was on was changing course. He fiddled around for a few minutes with the compass and assured me the veins were diverging, not converging.</p>

  <p>Two days later I walked back to scale down before mucking and a voice resounded from the face. "Jackson, you gawdam bastard." There was Whitey, shaking his head over a gawdawful pile of rock between us. "If you had told me you were lonesome I'd have come to visit," he cried. "You didn't have to make this damned mess."</p>

  <p>We ran my mucker around and cleaned up his drift with a word or two about the surveying techniques of the foreman.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 32. This "Whitey," given here as Vernon "Whitey" Miller, is not identified with the unnamed miner called "Whitey" whose fatal fall was described in the barroom vignette following "Mine Is Not to Question Why" in Part III; the book does not say whether they are the same person, and its stories are not told in strict chronological order.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="third-stool-p33">Third Stool from the Rear</h2>

  <p>"You get to meet the bird that bought the Silver Dollar? He seems likable enough and his wife is a real looker...she'll draw some trade for him.</p>

  <p>"He got his initiation the first week he opened...someone warned him about Woody so he bought a baseball bat and hid it in a cubby hole at the end of the bar...Thursday night Woody comes in, has a beer and goes home...Friday night he comes in again, has a beer and goes home...Willie's thinking he's home safe and stops worrying then on Saturday night he comes in already loaded, orders a boilermaker and shoves Smitty off the stool he wants to set on.</p>

  <p>"Willie didn't like it but he sees that Smitty took it good natured so he gets his drink and tries to make small talk...by midnight the big bastard has run everybody off and Willie was afraid to use the bat after seeing how long his arms are and how everybody backed off from him.</p>

  <p>"Two o'clock Willie tells him he has to close...Woody says to pour him another and he'll go home...turns three o'clock and Willie's so damn tired he doesn't give a shit if Woody tromps his butt in a mudhole...tells him that's all, he ain't gonna serve him another drink. Old Woody rears up, throws his glass at the light over the sink and staggers out.</p>

  <p>"Another night like that for Willie and we'll probably see a for sale sign in the window."</p>

